Thoron: connect native ad blocking to page loading.

Nothing here happens unless the ThoronNativeAdblock feature is on.
- chrome/browser depends on components/thoron_adblock.
- ChromeContentBrowserClient puts the ad-blocking proxy first in front of every subresource
  URLLoaderFactory, so a blocked request never reaches an extension or the network. It skips sites where
  the person allowed ads (Chromium's per-site Ads setting, the native blocker's per-site switch).
- It tells the engine service where updated lists are kept (the user data dir) and to download them
  through the browser's own network context.
- Page info shows the Ads setting on every site while native ad blocking is on.
- While it is on, page info and Settings call that setting "Ads and trackers" and say what it does
  (new strings in components/site_settings_strings.grdp); with it off they keep upstream's wording.
- Under that row, page info says "Thoron blocks them unless you allow them on this site" instead of
  upstream's "Site shows intrusive or misleading ads", which would read as a warning about the site.
- When it blocks a request, the page is marked as having blocked ads, which shows the address bar icon
  (without upstream's expanding label, which would animate on most page loads);
  its bubble says how many ads and trackers were blocked on the page (counted in memory only) and has
  a checkbox that allows them on the site and reloads.
- Each document is sent what it needs when its navigation is ready to commit (a universal
  WebContentsObserver, on the frame's channel, the way the subresource filter activates a document): a
  style sheet that hides the site's elements and the site's scriptlets. A frame agent in the renderer
  inserts the style sheet as a user style sheet and runs the scriptlets in the page's world at document
  start, before any page script. Nothing is sent on a site where the person allowed ads.
- Generic hide rules ("##.ad-banner"): the frame agent tells the browser which class names and ids its
  document uses (when the DOM is ready, at load, and four more times in the next 15 seconds) and inserts
  the style sheet that comes back. The browser answers by the address the frame committed, with the
  site's exceptions applied, and not at all where ads are allowed.
- A URLLoaderRequestInterceptor on every navigation (WillCreateURLLoaderRequestInterceptors) looks at
  a document's request at the start and after every redirect: `$removeparam` parameters are taken out
  through an internal redirect, and a frame the lists block is refused. A URLLoaderThrottle on every
  document request (CreateURLLoaderThrottles) adds `$csp` policies to the response. Neither acts on a
  site where the person allowed ads.
- An isolated world id of its own for the runner of procedural hide rules, which the frame agent runs
  at document start.
- WillInterceptWebSocket and CreateWebSocket: while the feature is on, every WebSocket connection of a
  frame is put to the engine; a blocked one is dropped, any other goes on as before (to an extension
  that asked for it, or straight to the network).
- chrome://flags has a switch for it ("Built-in ad blocking", #thoron-native-adblock).
- chrome://adblock is its settings page: the filter lists on and off, lists a
  person adds by address, and their own rules.
- The installer ships the saved engine, the filter lists and the resources file.

